diff --git a/docs/protocol.org b/docs/protocol.org
index 93f6c42..6a4755d 100644
--- a/docs/protocol.org
+++ b/docs/protocol.org
@@ -686,6 +686,7 @@ Each option with its type, its default and what it means:
 - ~org_agenda_custom_commands~ :: object[], ~[]~. Commands of the agenda menu. Each has a ~key~ and a ~description~, and either a ~type~ (~agenda~, ~alltodo~, ~todo~, ~tags~ or ~tags-todo~) with a ~match~ (keywords such as ~NEXT|WAIT~ for ~todo~, a match string such as ~work-home~ or ~PRIORITY="A"~ for ~tags~), or ~blocks~, a list of such views shown one after the other. ~header~ replaces a view's header (empty: none), and ~settings~ holds agenda options for the command or a block (~org_agenda_span~, ~org_agenda_start_day~ …). A command with only a key and a description groups the commands whose keys start with its key. (~org-agenda-custom-commands~)
 - ~virtual_documents~ :: boolean, from the client's capabilities. Whether the client fetches the agenda with ~workspace/textDocumentContent~. A client that announces that capability need not set this.
 - ~show_document~ :: boolean, from the client's capabilities. Whether the client shows the documents the server asks it to show (~window/showDocument~): the agenda, capture buffers, an entry's file. A client that announces that capability need not set this; the Vim plugin does, for the LSP clients that leave the request to it.
+- ~create_files~ :: boolean, from the client's capabilities. Whether an edit may make the files it is for (a ~create~ resource operation), as a new archive file from the agenda; else the server writes them itself. The Vim plugin turns it off: coc.nvim has the empty file it makes as one empty line, which the edit's text would then end with.
 - ~org_client~ :: boolean, ~false~. The client has org-lsp's editor code: it knows the server's own token types and does what a command's result asks. A client that can announce it does so with ~experimental.orgClient~; the Vim, Sublime Text and Zed plugins set this. Without it, the server sends the standard token types of LSP, which editors color without being told, ~message_prompts~ is on, and so are more code actions when the client shows message requests (~window.showMessage~) or ~message_prompts~ is set.
 - ~message_prompts~ :: boolean, true for a client without org-lsp's editor code (~org_client~). Ask what commands ask for with ~window/showMessageRequest~ (a key, one of a list) or, for text and dates, through a rename at the cursor (~textDocument/rename~, whose new name is the answer), and say what they have to say with ~window/showMessage~, for a client that does nothing with a command's result.
 - ~line_highlights~ :: boolean, ~false~. Whether the client highlights lines of the agenda from edge to edge as the server sends them (~org-lsp/lineHighlights~): the entry the clock runs on. As a client announcing ~experimental.orgLineHighlights~ does: for the Vim plugin and the Sublime Text package.
