+* org-protocol
+:PROPERTIES:
+:CUSTOM_ID: org-protocol
+:END:
+
+~org-lsp protocol URL~ is what the system runs for an ~org-protocol://~ URL
+([[file:org-protocol.org][org-protocol.org]]). Every server listens for such URLs from the start, for
+any client, in ~protocol/~ of the state directory (~$XDG_STATE_HOME/org-lsp~,
+=~/.local/state/org-lsp=, =%LOCALAPPDATA%\org-lsp= on Windows; the
+directory only its owner may enter):
+
+- ~PID-ID.json~ :: ~{ "pid", "socket" }~: the Unix socket it listens on
+  (mode ~0600~); on Windows ~{ "pid", "address", "token" }~, a loopback
+  port and the token it asks for. Written again, at most every three
+  seconds, when the client sends anything: the subcommand tries the entry
+  written last first, and removes one whose server does not answer.
+  Removed when the server stops.
+- ~pending-TIME-PID.json~ :: ~{ "url", "time" }~: a URL left by the
+  subcommand for the server of the editor it started
+  (~org_protocol_editor~). A server takes those of the last two minutes a
+  second after it starts, renaming each first so that one server alone
+  takes it, and removes the others.
+- ~stored-links.jsonl~ :: a line ~["URL", "TITLE" or null]~ for each link
+  stored while no server ran; the next server to start takes them all
+  among its stored links.
+
+The subcommand writes one line of JSON, ~{ "url" }~ (with ~"token"~ on
+Windows), and the server answers with one: ~{ "message" }~, what Org said,
+which the subcommand prints, or ~{ "error" }~, which makes it fail. Only
+URLs of the ~org-protocol:~ scheme are taken, of at most 256 KiB, sent
+within two seconds.
+
