From e4988d8e296730b7db58d1b09a5086b0fabcb210 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 10:32:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 47/88] Programs stop at a size, downloads go one at a time as
 written

- Programs (man, info, ctags, dot, shell: links) are read up to a size
  (16 MB; 1 MB for a shell: link, whose message shows its start) and
  stopped there, their process group killed with libc::kill rather than
  the kill program; on Windows the shell gets its line as it is
  (raw_arg), and the module says only the program itself is stopped there.
- Remote images: curl takes the URL as written (--globoff); a download
  over 20 MB fails though curl did not stop it; the previews download one
  after the other, and one whose document changed or closed before its
  turn is skipped; the images of files are answered at once and the
  preview comes again with the downloads; nothing is said of a preview
  whose document changed; a long URL makes a short enough file name.
- A man: link naming a file (man:/etc/hostname) is refused, as one
  giving an option is.
- org-protocol open-source refuses a working directory that is empty or
  relative; the socket goes to /tmp only when its name is too long, in
  the directory of the server's own user.
- docs/plugins.org says org.roam.extract refuses a file name that asks
  a question. A recorded graph case with cite: links and ROAM_REFS shows
  org-roam 2.3.1 draws a [[cite:key]] link as a link to its path, and no
  citation as an edge, as org-lsp does.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01F8WHf8fTvjUpFp8gNQrPFQ
