diff --git a/docs/protocol.org b/docs/protocol.org
index 56f49b5..f2679c5 100644
--- a/docs/protocol.org
+++ b/docs/protocol.org
@@ -495,7 +495,7 @@ Each option with its type, its default and what it means:
 - ~emacs~ :: string, ~"emacs"~. The Emacs program, for what only Org itself does: export (~org.export.dispatch~). The server starts it (~emacs --batch -Q~) when that is first asked for and keeps it running, so that Org loads once. Without Emacs, everything else works as before.
 - ~emacs_config_file~ :: string, none. A file of Emacs Lisp that Emacs loads before Org, for the variables export reads (~org-export-backends~, ~org-html-…~, ~org-latex-…~, ~org-publish-project-alist~): a file of its own, or your init file. =~/= is the home directory.
 - ~emacs_config~ :: string, none. Emacs Lisp that Emacs evaluates after ~emacs_config_file~ and before loading Org, for a few settings without a file that ~emacs_options~ cannot say: a function or a hook, or ~(add-to-list 'load-path "~/src/org-mode/lisp")~ for another Org. A yes-or-no question Emacs asks while it works (~org-confirm-babel-evaluate~) is asked in the editor.
-- ~emacs_options~ :: { [option]: value }, ~{}~. Org's options for what Emacs does, by their names in Org, which Emacs sets as Customize does: ~{ "org-attach-method": "mv", "org-export-with-toc": false }~. ~true~ is ~t~ and ~false~ ~nil~, a list is a list and a table an association list; a string is a symbol where the option takes one. Each is set when Emacs loads the library it is in, after ~emacs_config_file~ and ~emacs_config~, and so wins over them. A value the option does not take is left out, with a message. The options are those marked ~emacs~ in the Options section of [[file:parity.org][docs/parity.org]]; the others are settings of the server, or Lisp (functions, hooks), which goes in ~emacs_config~.
+- ~emacs_options~ :: { [option]: value }, ~{}~. Org's options for what Emacs does, by their names in Org, which Emacs sets as Customize does: ~{ "org-attach-method": "mv", "org-export-with-toc": false }~. ~true~ is ~t~ and ~false~ ~nil~, a list is a list and a table an association list; a string is a symbol where the option takes one. Each is set when Emacs loads the library it is in, after ~emacs_config_file~ and ~emacs_config~, and so wins over them. A value the option does not take is left out, with a message. The options, each with Org's documentation of it, are in [[file:emacs-options.org][docs/emacs-options.org]]; the others are settings of the server, or Lisp (functions, hooks), which goes in ~emacs_config~.
 - ~auto_save~ :: boolean, ~true~. Save the files a command changes other than the one it was run in: an entry's file changed from the agenda, the target of a capture or of a refile. The editor saves them once it has made the change. Off, they are left with unsaved changes, as Emacs leaves them.
 - ~org_cycle_separator_lines~ :: number, ~2~. With at least this many blank lines after a subtree, the last one stays outside its fold. Negative: all of them stay outside. ~0~: none does. (~org-cycle-separator-lines~)
 - ~org_cycle_include_plain_lists~ :: true, false, "integrate", ~true~. Whether list items fold, as subtrees do. ~integrate~ makes them part of the outline: TAB showing a headline's children folds its lists to their top items too. ~false~: they do not fold. (~org-cycle-include-plain-lists~)
